add call_id to accepted_call_ids list.\n5. For diagnostics, for each call (in order), create object: accepted (boolean), call_id, normalized_gain (rounded? Not specified rounding, but output schema expects number. The instruction says \"Return rounded per-call diagnostics.\" So we should round. To what precision? Not given. Maybe to some default? Possibly to avoid floating issues, we can round to something like 6 decimal places? But the output schema says \"number\", so any float is acceptable, but rounding might be needed? In the data, values are floats. The instruction says \"rounded per-call diagnostics\". I'll round to, say, 6 decimal places using round(value, 6). Or maybe just keep as float? To be safe, I'll round to 6 digits. Also ensure we use the normalized_gain that was computed even if not accepted.\n\n6. Return dict with accepted_call_ids list and diagnostics list.\n\nImportant: The function must not mutate case. So we will iterate over copy.\n\nAlso note: The output schema requires accepted_call_ids as array of strings, diagnostics as array of objects with accepted, call_id, normalized_gain. All required.\n\nMake sure to convert normalized_gain to float. Use Python's decimal? But we'll just use float.\n\nNow write solve(case) that returns dict.\n\nImplementation string should be Python source.\n\nI'll write:\n\n```python\ndef solve(case):\n    threshold = case['threshold']\n    calls = case['calls']\n    accepted = []\n    diagnostics = []\n    for c in calls:\n        gain = c['loss_without_call'] - c['loss_with_call']\n        # normalize by prediction_tokens (assume >0)\n        norm_gain = gain / c['prediction_tokens'] if c['prediction_tokens'] > 0 else 0.0\n        # round to 6 decimals\n        norm_gain_rounded = round(norm_gain, 6)\n        accepted_flag = c['parseable'] and norm_gain >= threshold\n        if accepted_flag:\n            accepted.append(c['call_id'])\n        diagnostics.append({\n            'accepted': accepted_flag,\n          